te's incumbency, name recognition, and business-community backing outweigh New Hampshire's slight Democratic lean", "Warmington's affordability and cannabis messaging has not visibly moved the numbers"], "rationale": "Polling is recent, plentiful, and consistent, with every survey since August showing Ayotte ahead by four to sixteen points and the latest credible polls at six to eight. Expert ratings and prediction markets align on a Republican favorite, leaving Warmington only a narrow path.", "watch": "A late shift on affordability or school funding, or a turnout surprise in a low-turnout midterm, could tighten the race.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Incumbent Kelly Ayotte (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Kelly Ayotte enters the final week of her re-election race as a clear favorite over Democrat Cinde Warmington. Polling since late summer has consistently shown her ahead, with the most recent surveys from Saint Anselm College, UMass Lowell, and Rasmussen putting her lead between six and ten points. Independent ratings mostly call the race Likely R, and prediction markets give Warmington only about a seven percent chance.\n\nWarmington has campaigned on affordability, school funding, and marijuana legalization, and the candidates met in several late-October debates, but there is little evidence she has closed the gap. Ayotte retains strong name recognition from her time as senator and attorney general, along with visible business-community support. New Hampshire's slight Democratic lean and the possibility of a late shift keep this from being a Safe R rating, but the evidence points firmly toward the incumbent.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0651, "margin": -8.09}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0566, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0654, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0669, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0672, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.0698, "margin": -8.0